     "checking the hallways, and quietly keeping an eye out for anything that might need quick attention. He also "
     "helps staff the neighborhood's small volunteer emergency line, so residents can reach him even when he is "
     "technically off duty. Between the two of them, almost every kind of everyday emergency eventually comes up. "
     "Sometimes Ms Lan is calling because her smoke alarm went off over burnt toast, because she smells something "
     "odd near the stove, or because she tripped on a wet step and twisted her ankle a little. On other days she "
     "is locked out of her own apartment after rushing out without her keys, dealing with a flickering light "
     "during a power cut, or trying to figure out what to do about a flat tire in the parking garage after dark. "
     "Mr Fairmont, for his part, has learned that most small emergencies calm down quickly once someone walks "
     "through the next step clearly and without panic. He shows residents where the fire extinguisher and the "
     "first-aid kit are kept, explains which noises from the elevator are normal and which ones mean he should "
     "come take a look, and keeps a card with the local emergency number taped inside the front desk drawer just "
     "in case anyone forgets it. None of their conversations are dramatic, and most of them follow a familiar, "
     "practical shape: a clear description of what just happened, a calm next step, and a simple way to make sure "
     "it does not happen again. A burst pipe gets reported and shut off quickly, a child briefly separated from a "
     "parent gets found within minutes, a stuck elevator gets a patient voice on the intercom, and a minor burn "
     "gets a cool cloth and some simple advice. This is what everyday emergency conversation sounds like between "
     "two people who have learned, case by case, that staying calm and specific almost always helps more than "
     "rushing or guessing. On any given week they might be talking about a mild allergic reaction after dinner, a "
     "suspicious noise outside at night, or a storm warning that has everyone checking their windows twice. Ms "
     "Lan has learned that naming exactly what is wrong always helps first, while Mr Fairmont has learned that "
     "most people, once they know someone capable is listening, handle a small emergency far more calmly than "
     "they expect to."
